.\" packaging/debian/irc-serve.1 -- the manual page lintian says /usr/bin/irc-serve
.\" is missing. Debian POLICY section 12.1 asks for one, lintian reports the absence as
.\" `no-manual-page`, and this file is the answer.
.\"
.\" WHY IT IS SHORT, and the cost that honesty imposes: `irc-serve --help` is 180 lines
.\" and is the program's own complete flag surface, kept next to the code that parses it.
.\" This page does not restate it -- a second description of the same flags is a second
.\" thing to rot, which is the argument packaging/debian/rules makes about NOT having a
.\" debian/install file. What is here instead is what `--help` does not lead with: what
.\" the program is, what a minimal invocation looks like, where the configuration is
.\" documented, and the two facts an operator has to know before the first connection --
.\" that this build has no transport encryption, and that a node with no --secret
.\" federates with nobody. Both are printed by --help and both are the kind of thing
.\" someone reads here.
.\"
.\" EVERY FACT BELOW IS COPIED FROM THE TREE, not from memory. The version comes from
.\" src/core/server.h's IRC_SERVE_VERSION; the flag names and defaults come from the
.\" --help text of the same commit this page ships in. scripts/check-docs-truth.py reads
.\" the version out of the same header and fails if the two disagree.
.TH IRC\-SERVE 1 "2026\-09\-05" "irc\-serve 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
irc\-serve \- federated modern\-spec IRC server in C
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B irc\-serve
.RI [ port ]
.RB [ \-\-name
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-secret
.IR S ]
.RB [ \-\-peer
.IR NAME , HOST , PORT ]...
.RB [ \-\-sasl\-store
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-account\-store
.IR PATH ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B irc\-serve
is a single\-process IRC server implementing the modern IRC client protocol set, with
federation between nodes over the
.B FEDERATE
handshake. It is written in C99 and POSIX with no runtime dependency beyond the C
library, so a packaged build links against glibc alone.
.PP
With no arguments it listens on port 6667 as
.BR irc.test .
A
.I port
of 0 asks the kernel for an ephemeral port and the node reports which one it chose on
its startup line, which is what the test suite does.
.SH OPTIONS
Every flag, its grammar, its default and its failure mode is printed by
.B "irc\-serve \-\-help"
and that text is the authoritative surface.
.B \-\-help
exits 0 rather than starting a node.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
This node's server name. Default
.BR irc.test .
.TP
.BI \-\-secret " S"
The shared federation secret a peer must present in
.BR FEDERATE .
Default is empty, which means this node REFUSES inbound peers: with no secret it
federates with nobody, and a claim is rejected as
.B reason=NO_SECRET
rather than as a wrong secret.
.TP
.BI \-\-peer " NAME,HOST,PORT"
A peer to link with, repeatable up to 16. Configure each pair in ONE direction: a link
establishes when either side connects, so two nodes that both list each other will both
dial and neither will accept.
.SH NOTES
.SS Transport encryption
The Debian package is configured with
.B \-DWITH_TLS=OFF
and therefore has NO transport encryption at all: no
.B tls
or
.B sts
capability is advertised and
.B STARTTLS
is answered 691. The TLS implementation is in the source tree and is not compiled into
this build. See SECURITY.md in the source tree, which describes the transport layer and
what a build that includes it requires.
.SS Peer links and TLS, in the source tree build
The
.BR \-\-tls\-cert ,
.BR \-\-tls\-key ,
.BR \-\-tls\-ca ,
.BR \-\-tls\-require ,
.BR \-\-tls\-port ,
.BR \-\-peer\-tls ,
.BR \-\-tls\-insecure ,
.BR \-\-tls\-ocsp\-staple
and
.BR \-\-tls\-staple\-permissive
flags exist in a
.BR \-DWITH_TLS=ON
build and are refused or absent in this one.
.B \-\-peer\-tls
enforces TLS on the direction THIS NODE DIALS and cannot prevent a peer from dialling
this node's plaintext port; that is what
.B \-\-tls\-require
and
.B \-\-tls\-port
are for.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/irc\-serve/changelog.Debian.gz
The packaging changelog.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/irc\-serve/copyright
The licence, AGPL\-3.0\-or\-later. The full text is in
.IR /usr/share/common\-licenses/AGPL\-3 .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B \-\-help
exits 0. A configuration error exits non\-zero with a named reason on stderr, and a node
that cannot bind or cannot load its stores does not start.
.SH SEE ALSO
The project README, SECURITY.md and docs/SPEC_TRACKING.md in the source tree, and
.B "irc\-serve \-\-help"
for the complete flag surface.
.SH BUGS
Report at https://github.com/TheFirstIstari/irc\-serve/issues